    1312	## §4. Negative-Shape Edges — Family-Level Closures
    1313	
    1314	The strongest project edges are **family-level closures**: E7.10 closes the
    1315	AKS family, E7.11 closes the convergence-acceleration family, E5.8 closes
    1316	the Brandt family. Each is a single argument that closes dozens of
    1317	individual approaches. Producing more of these is high-leverage.
    1318	
    1319	### N1 — Tensor-network compression family — **BUILT (S77)**
    1320	**Hypothesis:** No tensor network of polylog bond dimension can represent
    1321	χ_P (for any reshape, any tensor-train ordering, any matrix product
    1322	structure). E2.1 is the W=primorial special case; E1.9 is the φ(x,a) 2D case;
    1323	E6.3 is the wavelet/DCT case. Conjecture a unified statement: "any
    1324	factorisation of χ_P with bond dimension polylog(N) requires
    1325	exponentially-many factors."
    1326	**Why novel:** the individual cases are each known; the family-level
    1327	statement is not.
    1328	**First step:** State the conjecture precisely. Test it on 3-4 new tensor
    1329	factorisations (CP, Tucker, hierarchical Tucker, MERA).
    1330	**Outcome (S77):** Family-level closure verified empirically across **22
    1331	(W, d) pairs** spanning W ∈ {2,3,4,5,6,7}, d ∈ {4,6,8,10,12}. Five
    1332	classical ansätze (MPS, Hierarchical Tucker root-children, PEPS 2D
    1333	boundary, Tensor Ring, CP-rank Kruskal LB) are reduced to E2.1's
    1334	unfolding-rank lower bound — half-cut bond dim is **identical** across
    1335	all five and equals `min(W^j, φ(W)·W^(d-j-1)+1)` (exact saturation in
    1336	21/22 cases; one finite-size deficit of 1 at W=5, d=4). Tucker and MERA
    1337	close by orthogonal mechanisms (mode-slice independence; parameter
    1338	count). Asymptotic ratio `bond_dim / sqrt(N) → φ(W)/W` (the **Mertens
    1339	product**) — verified at d=12 for W∈{2,3,4} and at d=8 for W=6: 0.515
    1340	vs 0.500; 0.668 vs 0.667; 0.501 vs 0.500; 0.334 vs 0.333. **Net new
    1341	content**: (i) E2.1 lifted from single-ansatz to family-level scope —
