| §F6 / §P7 — does the dyadic family π(2^k), k = 1..N, admit per-query polylog amortisation beyond generic-magnitude HKM? (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES F6 / OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS P7 per-query side) | EXPERIMENT (B-NEGATIVE: three independent structural tests on (π(2^k))_{k=1..56} all return random-baseline values within Monte Carlo noise) | E (E1.1 + E1.5; refines E1.1 inline) | S246 novelty mode. Built `experiments/wildcard/dyadic_pi_structure/dyadic_pi_structure.py`: (1) hard-coded OEIS A007053 reference values π(2^k) for k=0..56, verified k≤18 against sympy.primepi (18/18 exact); (2) computed Li(2^k) and Riemann R(2^k) at 80-decimal mpmath precision, residual r_R(k) = π(2^k) − R(2^k), normalised c_R(k) = r_R(k)/2^{k/2}; (3) Monte Carlo random-binary BM linear-complexity null at length 56 with matched n_pos=25 / n_neg=31 split (4000 shuffles): mean=28.25, std=1.01, p05=27, p95=30; data BM(sign(r_R))=28, sits at 0.25σ below MC mean — F1 (BM ≤ MC p05) FAILS; (4) MC iid-Gaussian lag-1 |autocorr| null (4000 trials): p95=0.250, p99=0.328, p999=0.437; data max |ac| over lags 1..10 = 0.283 at lag 1, below MC p99, Bonferroni-corrected over 10 lags p ≈ 0.4 — F2 (max |ac| ≥ MC p999) FAILS; (5) HKM cost test via fresh subprocess sympy.primepi with no sieve-cache reuse: median T(2^k) / mean(T(2^k − 1), T(2^k + 1)) = 0.998 / 1.047 / 0.995 at k = 28, 30, 32 (avg 1.013) — F3 (dyadic speedup ratio ≤ 0.5) FAILS. **All three F-claims FAIL → B-NEGATIVE per-query closure.** Note: an earlier v1 result with Li-baseline gave spurious lag-1 = 0.87 driven by the smooth -Li(√x)/2 Möbius-series correction; switching to R-baseline (which absorbs the smooth Σ μ(n)/n Li(x^{1/n}) series) eliminates the confound. **Structural reason** (two independent mechanisms): (a) information-theoretic — the explicit-formula zero-sum −Σ_ρ R(x^ρ) ≈ −2√x Σ_n cos(γ_n log x − arg(ρ_n))/|ρ_n| has phases γ_n · k log 2 mod 2π Weyl-equidistributed in k for every γ_n with γ_n irrational, independently of the dyadic form; (b) algorithmic — Lucy / Meissel-Lehmer outer loop processes {n ≤ √x} and x^{2/3}-smooth counts which carry no binary-representation side-channel. **What this rules out**: (i) any algorithm computing π(2^k) substantially faster than generic-x π(x) at the same magnitude; (ii) any compressed representation of (π(2^k))_k of size below the trivial Σ_k k = O(N²) output bit-budget; (iii) any short-recurrence/closed-form for residuals r_R(k) at the n=56 scale. **What this does NOT close**: cross-k batched amortisation (Thread-5 / Correlation-Dichotomy shape transposed to dyadic family) — the shared zero database (γ_n)_{n=1..K} is k-independent and admits per-query O(K) cost with K = polylog. Registered as F6.b in NOVELTY_CHALLENGES.md and P7.b in OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md. **Successor challenges proposed in S246**: F6.a (cross-modulus generalisation x_k = m^k for m ∈ {3, 5, 6, 10, 30}), F6.b (batched-on-k amortisation), F6.c (rigorous Ω(N²) lower bound from output bit-budget). **Cross-domain ingredient**: Berlekamp-Massey linear complexity over GF(2) (signal-processing standard). CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md addition: "Berlekamp-Massey + Monte Carlo random-shuffle null for testing low-complexity linear recurrences in arithmetic sequences — USED-E (S246), confirmed n=56 too short to discriminate polylog from random; minimum useful length n > log²(n) · 5 ≈ 200". **Cites** E1.1 (per-query HKM x^{2/3} bound; refined inline with dyadic per-query no-amortisation note), E1.5 (per-step 0.537 entropy; consistent with Weyl-equidistribution mechanism). See `experiments/wildcard/dyadic_pi_structure/{dyadic_pi_structure.py, dyadic_pi_structure_results.md, raw_data.json, hkm_cost.json, summary.json}`, `archive/sessions/session246_f6_dyadic_pi_structure.md`. | 246 |
